<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucretius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 99–c. 55 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Titus Lucretius Carus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucretius, in full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> Lucretius Carus, was a Roman poet and philosopher of the first century BCE. His poem On the Nature of Things (De rerum natura) set out the physics and ethics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a>, arguing that the universe consists of atoms and void and that fear of the gods and of death is groundless. Rediscovered in 1417, it influenced early modern science and philosophy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucretius.jpg" alt="Lucretius" width="320" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Designed by “T. C.” (possibly Thomas Creech, died 1700); drawn and engraved by M. Burghers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucretius_pointing_to_the_casus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
